<%# The top bar (spec §5.3): the page's breadcrumb when it supplies one via content_for :crumbs,
    else its title; and the search box, which posts to the sitewide SearchController (stage 2,
    S2-5) rather than to the Files page it used to. The Files page keeps its OWN search in its
    own toolbar (files/index.html.erb's .files-search) for the flat, files-only query FilesController
    already answers. %>
<header class="topbar">
  <%# BACK, THE ONE BACK BUTTON (2026-11-24): on every signed-in page, at the far left, and it does
      what the browser's own Back does — one step back in this tab's history, so the page returns as
      it was left (back_controller.js). The href is where it goes when nothing of ours is behind
      this page (ShellHelper#topbar_back_path), and with none it is drawn disabled. No prefetch: a
      hover would fetch a page the click never loads, and some of them run git. %>
  <% back_path = topbar_back_path %>
  <%= link_to (back_path || request.fullpath), class: "topbar-back", title: "Back",
              aria: { label: "Back", disabled: (back_path ? nil : "true") },
              data: { controller: "back", action: "back#go", turbo_prefetch: "false" } do %><%= rail_icon(:back) %><% end %>
  <div class="topbar-crumbs">
    <% if content_for?(:crumbs) %>
      <%= content_for(:crumbs) %>
    <% else %>
      <span class="topbar-title"><%= page_title_text %></span>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <%# A TURBO VISIT, NOT A PAGE LOAD (2026-11-24). It carried `turbo: false` since the shell landed,
      with no reason recorded. This box is on every page, and a tool left for another page is kept
      alive in the document's memory (lib/kept_tools.js): a search that loaded a new document from a
      tool's page cost the maker the tool they had stepped away from. search/index's own box is the
      same form and moved with it. %>
  <%= form_with url: search_path, method: :get, class: "topbar-search", role: "search" do %>
    <%= rail_icon(:search) %>
    <%# ALWAYS BLANK, DELIBERATELY -- @q IS ALSO THE NAME FilesController, InventoryItemsController
        AND SearchController ITSELF GIVE THEIR OWN IVARS, and echoing whichever one the current
        request happens to carry would leak one page's private local filter (Files', Inventory's)
        into a box that now means something else (search EVERYTHING). SearchController's OWN page
        already has its own big, sticky search-toolbar input for that job (search/index.html.erb) —
        this is the entry point, not a second display of the last query. rail_controller.js's
        Ctrl/Cmd-K handler finds this field by `.topbar-search input[type=search]`, unchanged.
        The hint is per platform (ShellHelper#search_shortcut, UX audit A7): it was "⌘K" on every OS. %>
    <input type="search" name="q" placeholder="Search everything" aria-label="Search everything"
           aria-keyshortcuts="<%= ShellHelper::SEARCH_KEYS %>" autocomplete="off">
    <kbd class="topbar-kbd" aria-hidden="true"><%= search_shortcut %></kbd>
  <% end %>
  <%# NEW ▾ (structural redesign spec §4.2): everything here creates where the maker is standing —
      NewMenuHelper#new_menu_folder_node_id. Upload sits beside it (layouts/_topbar_upload.html.erb),
      and both create in FolderDestination's folder. ON A PHONE (spec §4.3, slice 7) the summary reads
      "+" and Upload moves inside it: shell.css hides the top bar's Upload BUTTON at 719px and below and
      shows the Upload item here, which forwards a click to that button (upload_forward_controller.js). %>
  <details class="menu topbar-new" data-controller="menu">
    <summary class="btn" aria-label="New"><span class="topbar-new-label">New <span aria-hidden="true">▾</span></span><span class="topbar-new-plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
    <div class="menu-list">
      <%# PHONE ONLY (shell.css hides it above 719px): the top bar's Upload, from inside the one "+".
          It clicks the top bar's Upload BUTTON, never the file input (upload_forward_controller.js). %>
      <button type="button" class="menu-item topbar-new-upload" data-controller="upload-forward"
              data-action="upload-forward#open">Upload…</button>
      <div class="menu-sep topbar-new-upload" role="separator"></div>
      <%= form_with url: library_folders_path, method: :post, class: "menu-form", data: { turbo: false } do %>
        <%# `.to_s`: no folder is the library root, sent as "" exactly as Upload sends it. %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag :parent_node_id, new_menu_folder_node_id.to_s, id: nil %>
        <label class="menu-form-label" for="new_folder_name">New folder</label>
        <span class="menu-form-row">
          <%= text_field_tag :name, nil, id: "new_folder_name", placeholder: "Folder name",
                             required: true, autocomplete: "off" %>
          <%= submit_tag "Create", class: "small", name: nil %>
        </span>
      <% end %>
      <div class="menu-sep" role="separator"></div>
      <%= link_to "Text file", new_files_text_file_path(parent_node_id: new_menu_folder_node_id), class: "menu-item" %>
      <%= link_to "Project", new_project_path, class: "menu-item" %>
      <%# THE TOOL ENTRIES ARRIVE ONLY WHEN THE MENU OPENS. A lazy frame inside a closed <details> has
          no box, so Turbo's IntersectionObserver fires on open and not before: the layout itself never
          reads the addon registry (docs/invariants/addons.md), and an addon-source outage costs this
          menu its tool list instead of costing every page its render. `target="_top"` so a tool link
          navigates the page, not the frame. %>
      <turbo-frame id="new-menu-tools" src="<%= tools_new_menu_path(in: new_menu_folder_node_id) %>" loading="lazy" target="_top"></turbo-frame>
      <div class="menu-sep" role="separator"></div>
      <%= link_to "Inventory item", new_inventory_item_path, class: "menu-item" %>
    </div>
  </details>
  <%= render "layouts/topbar_upload" %>
</header>
